----- stdout -----
Report which instructions can never run in parallel.

Solana runs two transactions in parallel only when neither write-locks an account the other locks. Pina reads the program source, sorts every instruction account into fixed, keyed, and caller-chosen addresses, and reports hotspots: fixed accounts, such as a PDA whose seeds are all constants, that serialize every instruction writing them.

Usage: pina locks [OPTIONS]

Options:
  -p, --project <DIR>
          Directory inside the project to discover. Defaults to the current directory

      --json
          Emit a stable machine-readable JSON document

      --deny-hotspots
          Exit with status 1 when a hotspot is not listed in `[locks] allow`

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Examples:
  pina locks
  pina locks --json
  pina locks --project ./programs/privacy_pool
  pina locks --deny-hotspots

Accepting a hotspot:
  List intentional hotspots by account name in `[locks] allow` in pina.toml. Allowed hotspots are still reported; an entry that names no hotspot is an error.

Exit status:
  1 when --deny-hotspots finds a hotspot that is not allowed, or when the analysis fails.

